Article 1 Private prosecution cases directly accepted by the People's Court include:
(1) Cases to be handled only upon complaint:
1. Cases of insult and defamation (as provided for in Article 246 of the Criminal Law, except where such conduct seriously endangers social order and national interests);
2. Cases of violent interference with the freedom of marriage (as provided for in Paragraph 1 of Article 257 of the Criminal Law);
3. Cases of maltreatment (as provided for in Paragraph 1 of Article 260 of the Criminal Law, except where the victim is unable to file a complaint, or is unable to file a complaint due to coercion or intimidation);
4. Cases of unlawful possession (as provided for in Article 270 of the Criminal Law).
(2) Minor criminal cases for which the People's Procuratorate has not initiated public prosecution and the victim has evidence to prove:
1. Cases of intentional injury (as provided for in Paragraph 1 of Article 234 of the Criminal Law);
2. Cases of unlawful intrusion into a residence (as provided for in Article 245 of the Criminal Law);
3. Cases of infringement upon freedom of correspondence (as provided for in Article 252 of the Criminal Law);
4. Cases of bigamy (as provided for in Article 258 of the Criminal Law);
5. Cases of abandonment (as provided for in Article 261 of the Criminal Law);
6. Cases of producing and selling fake or substandard commodities (as provided for in Section 1 of Chapter III of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law, except where such conduct seriously endangers social order and national interests);
7. Cases of infringing intellectual property rights (as provided for in Section 7 of Chapter III of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law, except where such conduct seriously endangers social order and national interests);
8. Cases provided for in Chapters IV and V of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law for which a sentence of fixed-term imprisonment of not more than three years may be imposed.
For the cases provided for in this item, where the victim directly initiates a lawsuit with the People's Court, the People's Court shall accept the case according to law. For any of them with insufficient evidence that may be accepted by the public security organ, or where the defendant may be sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment of not less than three years, the People's Court shall inform the victim to report the case to the public security organ, or transfer the case to the public security organ for filing and investigation.
(3) Cases in which the victim has evidence to prove that the defendant's infringement upon the victim's personal or property rights shall be investigated for criminal liability according to law, and has evidence to prove that the victim once filed a complaint, but the public security organ or the People's Procuratorate has not investigated the defendant's criminal liability.

Article 17 A Primary People's Court shall transfer to an Intermediate People's Court for trial a criminal case of first instance for which a sentence of life imprisonment or the death penalty may be imposed.
A Primary People's Court may request to transfer the following criminal cases of first instance to an Intermediate People's Court for trial:
(1) major and complex cases;
(2) novel and difficult cases;
(3) cases with universal guiding significance in the application of law.
Where a case needs to be transferred to an Intermediate People's Court for trial, the Primary People's Court shall, after submitting the matter to the president of the court for decision, file a written request for transfer no later than fifteen days before the expiration of the case trial period. The Intermediate People's Court shall make a decision within ten days after receiving the application. If the transfer is not approved, it shall issue a written decision disapproving the transfer, and the case shall be tried according to law by the People's Court that requested the transfer; if the transfer is approved, it shall issue a written decision approving the transfer and notify the People's Procuratorate at the same level in writing.

Article 27 Where judicial personnel fall under any of the following circumstances, they shall withdraw on their own initiative, and the party and his or her legal representative have the right to apply for their withdrawal:
(1) being a party to the case or a close relative of a party;
(2) having a personal interest in the case themselves or through their close relatives;
(3) having served as a witness, expert witness, defender, litigation agent, or interpreter in the case;
(4) having a close relative relationship with the defender or litigation agent in the case;
(5) having other interests with the parties to the case that may affect impartial adjudication.

Article 28 Where judicial personnel fall under any of the following circumstances, the party and his or her legal representative have the right to apply for their withdrawal:
(1) meeting with the parties, defenders, or litigation agents of the case in violation of provisions;
(2) recommending or introducing defenders or litigation agents for the parties to the case, or introducing lawyers or other persons to handle the case;
(3) demanding or accepting property or other benefits from the parties to the case or the persons entrusted by them;
(4) accepting banquet invitations from the parties to the case or the persons entrusted by them, or participating in activities paid for by them;
(5) borrowing money or property from the parties to the case or the persons entrusted by them;
(6) committing other improper acts that may affect impartial adjudication.

Article 36 Where the party and his or her legal representative apply for the withdrawal of procuratorial personnel appearing in court, the People's Court shall handle the matter according to the circumstances:
(1) Where the application for withdrawal falls under the circumstances provided for in Articles 29 and 30 of the Criminal Procedure Law, the court shall decide to adjourn and notify the People's Procuratorate to make a decision as soon as possible;
(2) Where the application for withdrawal does not fall under the circumstances provided for in Articles 29 and 30 of the Criminal Procedure Law, the court shall reject it in court, and no reconsideration may be applied for.

Article 40 In adjudicating cases, a People's Court shall fully protect the right to defense enjoyed by defendants according to law.
In addition to exercising the right to defense himself or herself, a defendant may also retain a defender to defend. The following persons shall not serve as defenders:
(1) persons currently serving criminal punishments or under probation or parole;
(2) persons whose personal freedom is deprived or restricted according to law;
(3) persons who have been dismissed from public office or whose lawyer's or notary's practice certificates have been revoked;
(4) current staff members of People's Courts, People's Procuratorates, supervisory organs, public security organs, state security organs, or prisons;
(5) people's assessors;
(6) persons who have an interest in the adjudication result of the case;
(7) foreigners or stateless persons;
(8) persons without or with limited capacity for conduct.
Where a person specified in items (3) to (7) of the preceding paragraph is a guardian or close relative of the defendant and is retained by the defendant to serve as a defender, the retention may be permitted.

Article 47 With respect to the following defendants who have not retained defenders, the People's Court shall notify the legal aid institution to assign a lawyer to provide defense for them:
(1) blind, deaf, or mute persons;
(2) mental patients who have not completely lost the ability to recognize or control their own conduct;
(3) persons who may be sentenced to life imprisonment or the death penalty.
Where, in reviewing a death penalty case, the Higher People's Court finds that the defendant has not retained a defender, it shall notify the legal aid institution to assign a lawyer to provide defense for him or her.
The provisions of the preceding two paragraphs shall apply to cases of intentional crimes committed during the period of death sentence with a two-year reprieve.

Article 48 Under any of the following circumstances, where the defendant has not retained a defender, the People's Court may notify the legal aid institution to assign a lawyer to provide defense for him or her:
(1) in a joint crime case, other defendants have already retained defenders;
(2) the case has major social impact;
(3) the People's Procuratorate has filed a protest;
(4) the defendant's conduct may not constitute a crime;
(5) other circumstances where it is necessary to assign a lawyer to provide defense.

Article 72 The facts of a case to be proved by evidence include:
(1) the identities of the defendant and the victim;
(2) whether the alleged crime exists;
(3) whether the alleged crime was committed by the defendant;
(4) whether the defendant has criminal liability capacity, whether there is culpability, and the motive and purpose of committing the crime;
(5) the time, place, means, consequences, and causes of the crime, etc.;
(6) whether it is a joint crime or whether the criminal facts are related, and the defendant's status and role in the crime;
(7) whether the defendant has circumstances for severe punishment, light punishment, mitigated punishment, or exemption from punishment;
(8) the facts concerning the disposition of property involved in the case;
(9) the facts concerning the incidental civil action;
(10) procedural facts such as those concerning jurisdiction, withdrawal, and adjournment;
(11) other facts related to conviction and sentencing.
The standard of proof of reliable and sufficient evidence shall apply to finding the defendant guilty and imposing severe punishment on the defendant.

Article 80 The following persons shall not serve as witnesses:
(1) persons who have physiological or mental defects or are young, lacking the corresponding ability to discern or the ability to express correctly;
(2) persons who have an interest in the case and may affect the fair handling of the case;
(3) staff members of supervisory, public security, or judicial organs, or persons employed by them, who exercise supervisory investigation or criminal litigation functions such as inspection, examination, search, seizure, or organizing identification.
The People's Court may review whether a witness falls under the categories in the preceding paragraph by examining the witness's name, type and number of identification document, contact information, permanent residence information registration form, and other materials recorded in the relevant records.
Where due to objective reasons no qualified person can serve as a witness, the circumstances shall be noted in the record materials, and the relevant activities shall be recorded in full by audio and video.

Article 82 The following contents shall be examined with emphasis with respect to physical evidence and documentary evidence:
(1) whether the physical or documentary evidence is the original object or original document, and whether it has been identified or appraised; whether photographs, videos, or reproductions of physical evidence or copies or duplicates of documentary evidence are consistent with the original object or original document, whether they were made by two or more persons, and whether there are written explanations and signatures of the makers regarding the production process and the place where the original object or original document is stored;
(2) whether the collection procedures and methods of the physical and documentary evidence comply with laws and relevant regulations; whether physical and documentary evidence extracted or seized through inspection, examination, search, and seizure is accompanied by relevant records and lists; whether the records and lists are signed by the investigators, the holders of the items, and witnesses; if not signed, whether the reasons are stated; whether the names, characteristics, quantities, quality, etc., of the items are clearly stated;
(3) whether the physical and documentary evidence was damaged or altered during collection, custody, or appraisal;
(4) whether the physical and documentary evidence is related to the facts of the case; with respect to biological samples, traces, and items such as bloodstains, body fluids, hair, and fingerprints left at the scene that are related to the crime and meet the conditions for identification, whether DNA identification, fingerprint identification, etc., have been conducted, and whether they have been compared with the corresponding biological characteristics or items of the defendant or the victim;
(5) whether all physical and documentary evidence related to the facts of the case has been comprehensively collected.

Article 86 Physical or documentary evidence extracted or seized during inspection, examination, or search, if not accompanied by records or lists and the source of the physical or documentary evidence cannot be proved, shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case.
Where the collection procedures or methods of physical or documentary evidence have the following defects, the evidence may be admitted after correction or a reasonable explanation:
(1) the inspection, examination, search, or extraction records or seizure lists are not signed by the investigators, the holders of the items, or witnesses, or the names, characteristics, quantities, quality, etc., of the items are not clearly stated;
(2) photographs, videos, or reproductions of physical evidence, or copies or duplicates of documentary evidence, do not state that they are verified to be identical to the originals, do not state the reproduction time, or are not signed by the persons from whom they were collected or obtained;
(3) photographs, videos, or reproductions of physical evidence, or copies or duplicates of documentary evidence, do not have the producers' explanations of the production process and the storage location of the original object or document, or the explanations do not contain signatures;
(4) there are other defects.
Where there is doubt about the source or collection procedures of physical or documentary evidence and a reasonable explanation cannot be given, it shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case.

Article 87 With respect to witness testimony, the following contents shall be examined with emphasis:
(1) whether the content of the testimony was directly perceived by the witness;
(2) whether the witness's age at the time of testifying, cognitive, memory, and expressive abilities, and physiological and mental state affect testifying;
(3) whether the witness has an interest in the parties or the outcome of the case;
(4) whether witnesses were questioned individually;
(5) whether the making and revision of the interrogation record comply with laws and relevant regulations, whether the starting and ending times and place of the interrogation are stated, whether the witness was informed of relevant rights, obligations, and legal liabilities at the first interrogation, and whether the witness checked and confirmed the interrogation record;
(6) when questioning a minor witness, whether his legal representative or the appropriate adult as provided in Article 281, paragraph 1, of the Criminal Procedure Law was notified to be present, and whether the relevant person was present;
(7) whether there are circumstances of collecting witness testimony by illegal methods such as violence or threats;
(8) whether the testimony can corroborate each other and with other evidence, and whether there are contradictions; if there are contradictions, whether a reasonable explanation can be given.

Article 89 Witness testimony under any of the following circumstances shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) witnesses were not questioned individually;
(2) written testimony was not checked and confirmed by the witness;
(3) when questioning a deaf or mute person, a person who knows sign language should have been provided but was not;
(4) when questioning a witness who does not understand the local commonly used language or script, an interpreter should have been provided but was not.

Article 90 Where the collection procedures or methods of witness testimony have the following defects, the testimony may be admitted after correction or a reasonable explanation; if it cannot be corrected or reasonably explained, it shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) the interrogation record does not state the names of the interrogator, the recorder, and the legal representative, or the starting and ending times and place of the interrogation;
(2) the place of interrogation does not comply with regulations;
(3) the interrogation record does not record that the witness was informed of relevant rights, obligations, and legal liabilities;
(4) the interrogation record reflects that the same interrogator questioned different witnesses during the same time period;
(5) when questioning a minor, his legal representative or an appropriate adult was not present.

Article 93: For a defendant's confession and defense, the following contents shall be reviewed with emphasis:
(1) Whether the time, place, identity and number of interrogators, and the methods of interrogation, etc. conform to the law and relevant provisions;
(2) Whether the production and revision of the interrogation record conform to the law and relevant provisions, whether the specific start and end times and location of the interrogation are indicated, whether the defendant was informed of relevant rights and legal provisions at the first interrogation, and whether the defendant checked and confirmed the record;
(3) When interrogating a minor defendant, whether the legal representative or an appropriate adult was notified to be present, and whether the relevant persons were present;
(4) When interrogating a female minor defendant, whether female staff members were present;
(5) Whether there are circumstances of collecting the defendant's confession by illegal methods such as extorting confession by torture;
(6) Whether the defendant's confessions are consistent, whether there are repetitions, and the reasons for such repetitions;
(7) Whether all of the defendant's confessions and defenses are transferred with the case;
(8) Whether the content of the defendant's defense conforms to the facts of the case and common sense, and whether there are contradictions;
(9) Whether the defendant's confessions and defenses can corroborate with the confessions and defenses of co-defendants and other evidence, and whether there are contradictions; if there are contradictions, whether they can be reasonably explained.
When necessary, the defendant's confessions and defenses may be reviewed in light of on-site law enforcement audio-video records, interrogation audio-video recordings, health examination records and records of the defendant entering and leaving the detention center, etc.

Article 94: Where a defendant's confession falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) The interrogation record has not been checked and confirmed by the defendant;
(2) When interrogating a deaf or mute person, a person proficient in sign language was required to be provided but was not provided;
(3) When interrogating a defendant who does not understand the local commonly used language or script, an interpreter was required to be provided but was not provided;
(4) When interrogating a minor, the legal representative or an appropriate adult was not present.

Article 95: Where an interrogation record has the following defects, it may be adopted if corrections are made or a reasonable explanation is provided; if corrections cannot be made or a reasonable explanation cannot be provided, it shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) The interrogation time, interrogation place, interrogator, recorder, legal representative, etc. filled in the interrogation record are erroneous or contradictory;
(2) The interrogator has not signed;
(3) The first interrogation record does not record that the interrogated person was informed of relevant rights and legal provisions.

Article 97: The following contents regarding an expert opinion shall be reviewed with emphasis:
(1) Whether the expert institution and the expert have legal qualifications;
(2) Whether the expert is under circumstances requiring recusal;
(3) Whether the source, acquisition, storage, and submission for examination of the sample materials conform to the law and relevant provisions, whether they are consistent with the contents recorded in relevant extraction records and seizure lists, and whether the sample materials are reliable;
(4) Whether the formal requirements of the expert opinion are complete, whether the reason for initiating the expert evaluation, the commissioning party, the expert institution, the requirements, process, method, date, and other relevant contents of the expert evaluation are indicated, and whether the expert institution affixed its seal and the expert signed;
(5) Whether the expert evaluation procedure conforms to the law and relevant provisions;
(6) Whether the process and method of the expert evaluation conform to the specification requirements of relevant professions;
(7) Whether the expert opinion is clear;
(8) Whether the expert opinion is relevant to the facts of the case;
(9) Whether the expert opinion contradicts other evidence such as inspection and examination records and related photographs; if there are contradictions, whether they can be reasonably explained;
(10) Whether the expert opinion was timely informed to relevant persons according to law, and whether the parties have objections to the expert opinion.

Article 98: Where an expert opinion falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) The expert institution does not have legal qualifications, or the matter of expert evaluation exceeds the business scope or technical conditions of the expert institution;
(2) The expert does not have legal qualifications, does not have relevant professional technology or professional title, or violates recusal provisions;
(3) The source of the submitted materials or samples is unknown, or due to contamination, the conditions for expert evaluation are not met;
(4) The object of expert evaluation is inconsistent with the submitted materials or samples;
(5) The expert evaluation procedure violates provisions;
(6) The process and method of the expert evaluation do not conform to the specification requirements of relevant professions;
(7) The expert evaluation document lacks a signature or seal;
(8) The expert opinion is not relevant to the facts of the case;
(9) Other circumstances that violate relevant provisions.

Article 102: The inspection and examination records shall be reviewed with emphasis on the following contents:
(1) Whether the inspection and examination were conducted according to law, whether the production of the records conforms to the law and relevant provisions, and whether the persons conducting the inspection and examination and the witnesses present signed or affixed their seals;
(2) Whether the inspection and examination records recorded the reasons for initiating the inspection and examination, the time and place of the inspection and examination, the persons present, the orientation of the scene, the surrounding environment, etc., and the positions and characteristics of objects, persons, corpses, etc. at the scene, as well as the process of the inspection and examination; whether the written records are consistent with the physical objects or drawings, photographs, and videos; whether the scene, objects, traces, etc. are forged or damaged; whether personal characteristics, injuries, and physiological states are disguised or changed, etc.;
(3) If supplementary inspection and examination are conducted, whether the reasons for the re-inspection and re-examination are stated, and whether the circumstances before and after the inspections and examinations are contradictory.

Article 105: Where an identification record falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) The identification was not conducted under the auspices of investigation personnel or investigators;
(2) The identifying person saw the identification target before the identification;
(3) The identification activities were not conducted separately;
(4) The identification target was not mixed with other objects with similar characteristics, or the number of objects provided for identification does not conform to the provisions;
(5) During the identification, the identifying person was given obvious suggestions or there was obvious suspicion of indicating the target;
(6) Other circumstances where relevant provisions are violated and the authenticity of the identification record cannot be confirmed.

Article 108: With respect to audio-visual materials, the following contents shall be reviewed with emphasis:
(1) Whether a description of the extraction process is attached, and whether the source is lawful;
(2) Whether they are originals, whether there are copies and the number of copies; if they are copies, whether a description of the reasons why the originals could not be obtained, the production process of the copies, and the location where the originals are stored is attached, and whether the producer and the original holder of the audio-visual materials signed;
(3) Whether there are circumstances during the production process that violate the law and relevant provisions, such as threatening or inducing the parties;
(4) Whether the identities of the producer and holder, and the time, place, conditions, and methods of production are clearly stated;
(5) Whether the content and production process are authentic, and whether there are circumstances such as editing, addition, or alteration;
(6) Whether the content is relevant to the facts of the case.
Where there is doubt about audio-visual materials, an expert evaluation shall be conducted.

Article 109: Where audio-visual materials fall under any of the following circumstances, they shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) They have been tampered with, forged, or their authenticity cannot be determined;
(2) There are doubts about the time, place, method, etc. of their production or acquisition, and a reasonable explanation cannot be provided.

Article 110: With respect to whether electronic data is authentic, the following contents shall be reviewed with emphasis:
(1) Whether the original storage medium is transferred; when the original storage medium cannot be sealed or is inconvenient to move, whether the reasons are stated, and whether the collection and extraction process, the storage location of the original storage medium, or the source of the electronic data, etc. are indicated;
(2) Whether it has special identifiers such as digital signatures or digital certificates;
(3) Whether the collection and extraction process can be reproduced;
(4) If there are circumstances such as addition, deletion, or modification, whether a description is attached;
(5) Whether the integrity can be guaranteed.

Article 111: With respect to whether electronic data is complete, review and verification shall be conducted according to the corresponding methods for protecting the integrity of electronic data:
(1) Reviewing the seizure and sealing status of the original storage medium;
(2) Reviewing the collection and extraction process of the electronic data and viewing the video recordings;
(3) Comparing the integrity check values of the electronic data;
(4) Comparing it with the backup electronic data;
(5) Reviewing the access operation logs after freezing;
(6) Other methods.

Article 112: With respect to whether the collection and extraction of electronic data is lawful, the following contents shall be reviewed with emphasis:
(1) Whether the collection and extraction of electronic data is conducted by no fewer than two investigation personnel or investigators, and whether the evidence collection methods conform to relevant technical standards;
(2) Whether the collection and extraction of electronic data is accompanied by records and lists, and signed or sealed by the investigation personnel, investigators, the holder of the electronic data, the provider, and the witnesses present; if there is no signature or seal, whether the reasons are indicated; whether the category, file format, etc. of the electronic data are clearly indicated;
(3) Whether qualified persons serve as witnesses present in accordance with relevant provisions, and whether the relevant activities are videotaped;
(4) Where electronic data is collected and extracted by technical investigation or investigative measures, whether strict approval procedures have been followed according to law;
(5) Where an examination of electronic data is conducted, whether the examination procedure conforms to relevant provisions.

Article 113: Where the collection and extraction procedure for electronic data has the following defects, it may be adopted if corrections are made or a reasonable explanation is provided; if corrections cannot be made or a reasonable explanation cannot be provided, it shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) The electronic data is not transferred in a sealed state;
(2) The records or lists are not signed or sealed by the investigation personnel or investigators, the holder of the electronic data, the provider, or the witnesses present;
(3) The name, category, format, etc. of the electronic data are not clearly indicated;
(4) There are other defects.

Article 114: Where electronic data falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall not be used as the basis for deciding the case:
(1) It has been tampered with, forged, or its authenticity cannot be determined;
(2) There are circumstances such as addition, deletion, or modification that affect the authenticity of the electronic data;
(3) Other circumstances where the authenticity of the electronic data cannot be guaranteed.

Article 117: Where the use of evidence materials collected through technical investigation or investigative measures may endanger the personal safety of relevant persons, or may produce other serious consequences, the following protective measures may be adopted:
(1) Using pseudonyms and other means to replace the personal information of investigation personnel, investigators, and relevant persons;
(2) Not specifically stating the technical equipment and technical methods used in the technical investigation or investigative measures;
(3) Other necessary protective measures.

Article 119: With respect to evidence materials collected through technical investigation or investigative measures, in addition to reviewing them according to the corresponding provisions in Sections 2 to 7 of this Chapter based on the type of evidence to which the relevant evidence materials belong, the following contents shall also be reviewed with emphasis:
(1) Whether the case targeted by the technical investigation or investigative measures conforms to the provisions of the law;
(2) Whether the technical investigation measures have undergone strict approval procedures and were submitted to the relevant organ for execution according to the provisions; whether the technical investigative measures have undergone strict approval procedures after the criminal case was filed;
(3) Whether the types, applicable objects, and time limits of the technical investigation or investigative measures taken are executed in accordance with the contents stated in the approval decision;
(4) Whether the evidence materials collected through technical investigation or investigative measures contradict other evidence; if there are contradictions, whether they can be reasonably explained.

Article 123: Confessions of a defendant collected by the following illegal methods shall be excluded:
(1) Confessions made against the defendant's will by causing the defendant unbearable pain through violent methods such as beating or illegal use of restraints, or through despicable means such as disguised corporal punishment;
(2) Confessions made against the defendant's will by causing the defendant unbearable pain through methods such as threatening with violence or seriously harming the lawful rights and interests of the defendant or his close relatives;
(3) Confessions of a defendant collected by illegal restriction of personal freedom such as unlawful detention.

Article 124: Where a defendant is made to make a confession by means of extorting confession by torture, and thereafter, under the influence of such torture, makes repeated confessions identical to that confession, such repeated confessions shall also be excluded, except under the following circumstances:
(1) During the investigation period, the supervisory organ or the investigating organ, based on a complaint, report, or its own discovery, confirms or cannot exclude that evidence was collected by illegal methods and accordingly replaces the investigation personnel or investigators, and when other investigation personnel or investigators interrogate the defendant again, they inform him of the relevant rights and the legal consequences of pleading guilty, and the defendant voluntarily confesses;
(2) During the period of examination for arrest, review for prosecution, and adjudication, when procuratorial personnel or judicial personnel interrogate the defendant, they inform him of litigation rights and the legal consequences of pleading guilty, and the defendant voluntarily confesses.

Article 138: Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court of second instance shall review the lawfulness of evidence collection and handle the matter in accordance with the Criminal Procedure Law and the relevant provisions of this Interpretation:
(1) The People's Court of first instance did not review the application by the party and his defender or litigation agent for exclusion of illegal evidence and used the evidence as the basis for deciding the case;
(2) The People's Procuratorate, or the defendant, the private prosecutor, or their legal representatives, dissatisfied with the conclusion of the investigation concerning the lawfulness of evidence collection made by the People's Court of first instance, file a protest or an appeal;
(3) The party and his defender or litigation agent discover relevant clues or materials only after the conclusion of the first instance and apply to the People's Court for exclusion of illegal evidence.

Article 140: Where there is no direct evidence, but the indirect evidence simultaneously meets the following conditions, the defendant may be found guilty:
(1) The evidence has been verified to be true;
(2) The evidence corroborates each other, and there are no irreconcilable contradictions or inexplicable doubts;
(3) All evidence in the case forms a complete chain of evidence;
(4) Determining the facts of the case based on the evidence is sufficient to exclude reasonable doubt, and the conclusion is exclusive;
(5) The reasoning using the evidence conforms to logic and experience.

Article 143: The following evidence shall be used with caution; where corroborated by other evidence, it may be admitted:
(1) Statements, testimony, and confessions made by victims, witnesses, and defendants who have physical or mental defects, experience certain difficulty in perceiving and expressing the facts of the case, but have not yet lost the ability to correctly perceive and express;
(2) Testimony favorable to the defendant given by witnesses who have a kinship or other close relationship with the defendant, or testimony unfavorable to the defendant given by witnesses who have an interest conflict with the defendant.

Article 151: Where release on bail pending investigation or trial is decided for the following defendants, the defendants may be ordered to provide one or two sureties:
(1) Those who are unable to pay the bail money;
(2) Those who are minors or have reached the age of seventy-five;
(3) Other defendants for whom it is inappropriate to collect bail money.

Article 164: Where a defendant released on bail pending investigation or trial falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall decide to arrest him:
(1) Intentionally committing a new crime;
(2) Attempting to commit suicide or escape;
(3) Destroying or falsifying evidence, interfering with witnesses' testimony, or colluding to make consistent statements;
(4) Retaliating against, intimidating, or harassing victims, witnesses, expert witnesses, informants, or accusers;
(5) After summons, failing to appear without justifiable reasons, thereby affecting the normal conduct of trial activities;
(6) Without authorization changing contact information or residence, rendering summons impossible and affecting the normal conduct of trial activities;
(7) Without approval leaving the city or county where he resides, affecting the normal conduct of trial activities, or twice without approval leaving the city or county where he resides;
(8) In violation of regulations entering specific places, meeting or communicating with specific persons, or engaging in specific activities, affecting the normal conduct of trial activities, or twice violating the relevant regulations;
(9) Other circumstances under which arrest shall be decided according to law.

Article 165: Where a defendant under residential surveillance falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall decide to arrest him:
(1) Having any of the circumstances provided in items (1) through (5) of the preceding article;
(2) Without approval leaving the place where residential surveillance is executed, affecting the normal conduct of trial activities, or twice without approval leaving the place where residential surveillance is executed;
(3) Without approval meeting others or communicating, affecting the normal conduct of trial activities, or twice without approval meeting others or communicating;
(4) Where the defendant was not arrested because of suffering from a serious disease, being unable to take care of himself, or because of pregnancy or nursing his or her own infant, and the disease has been cured or the nursing period has expired;
(5) Other circumstances under which arrest shall be decided according to law.

Article 169: Where an arrested defendant falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may change the compulsory measures:
(1) Suffering from a serious disease and being unable to take care of himself;
(2) Being pregnant or nursing his or her own infant;
(3) Being the sole supporter of a person who is unable to take care of himself.

Article 170: Where an arrested defendant falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall release him immediately; where necessary, compulsory measures may be changed according to law:
(1) The People's Court of first instance has sentenced the defendant to be innocent, not criminally responsible, or exempt from criminal punishment;
(2) The People's Court of first instance has sentenced the defendant to public surveillance, declared probation, or solely applied supplementary punishment, and the judgment has not yet taken legal effect;
(3) The period of custody of the defendant has already reached the term of sentence imposed on him by the People's Court of first instance;
(4) The case cannot be concluded within the time limit prescribed by law.

Article 180: In an incidental civil action, the persons who bear compensation liability according to law include:
(1) the criminal defendant and other joint infringers who have not been investigated for criminal liability;
(2) the guardians of the criminal defendant;
(3) the heirs of the estate of a criminal sentenced to death;
(4) in a joint crime case, the heirs of the estate of a defendant who dies before the conclusion of the case;
(5) other units and individuals who shall bear compensation liability according to law for the victim's material losses.
Where relatives or friends of the defendant in the incidental civil action voluntarily compensate on his or her behalf, it may be permitted.

Article 182: The conditions for initiating an incidental civil action are:
(1) the plaintiff meets the statutory conditions;
(2) there is a definite defendant;
(3) there are specific claims for compensation and facts and reasons; and
(4) the case falls within the scope of incidental civil actions accepted by the People's Court.

Article 213: The Primary People's Court, the Intermediate People's Court, and the Higher People's Court shall form collegial panels composed of judges and people's assessors to adjudicate the following first-instance criminal cases:
(1) cases involving group interests or public interests;
(2) cases widely concerned by the people or otherwise having relatively major social impact;
(3) cases with complex circumstances or other circumstances requiring the participation of people's assessors in adjudication.
The Primary People's Court, the Intermediate People's Court, and the Higher People's Court shall form seven-person collegial panels composed of judges and people's assessors to adjudicate the following first-instance criminal cases:
(1) cases in which a sentence of fixed-term imprisonment of not less than ten years, life imprisonment, or death penalty may be imposed and which have major social impact;
(2) cases involving land expropriation and demolition, ecological environment protection, food and drug safety, and which have major social impact;
(3) other cases with major social impact.

Article 218: For a case filed by public prosecution, the People's Court shall, after receiving the indictment (with eight copies, and five additional copies for each additional defendant) and the case files and evidence, examine the following contents:
(1) whether the case falls within the jurisdiction of this court;
(2) whether the indictment states the defendant's identity, whether he or she has been or is being subjected to criminal punishment, administrative penalty, or disciplinary action, the circumstances of being subjected to custody measures, the time, type, and place of detention of the compulsory measures taken, the time, place, means, and consequences of the crime, and other circumstances that may affect conviction and sentencing; if there are multiple criminal facts, whether the facts are separately listed in the indictment;
(3) whether the evidence materials proving the charged criminal facts and affecting sentencing have been transferred, including the legal instruments for taking technical investigation and investigative measures and the evidence materials collected;
(4) whether the defendant's illegal gains or other property involved in the case have been sealed, seized, or frozen, and whether the sealing, seizure, or freezing has exceeded the time limit; whether the property involved in the case has been transferred with the case and a list of the property involved has been attached; whether the ownership status of the property involved has been stated; whether relevant evidence materials have been provided regarding the disposal of the property involved;
(5) whether the victim's name, address, and contact information are listed; whether a list of witnesses and expert witnesses is attached; whether an application is made for the court to notify witnesses, expert witnesses, or persons with specialized knowledge to appear in court, and whether the names, genders, ages, occupations, addresses, and contact information of the relevant persons are listed; whether a list of witnesses, expert witnesses, or victims requiring protection is attached;
(6) if the parties have entrusted defenders or litigation agents or have received legal aid, whether the names, addresses, and contact information of the defenders or litigation agents are listed;
(7) whether an incidental civil action is initiated; if so, whether the names, addresses, contact information, etc. of the parties to the incidental civil action are listed, and whether relevant evidence materials are attached;
(8) whether all legal formalities and litigation documents of the supervision investigation, investigation, and review for prosecution procedures are complete;
(9) if the defendant admits guilt and accepts punishment, whether a sentencing recommendation has been proposed and materials such as the written undertaking to admit guilt and accept punishment have been transferred;
(10) whether there are circumstances provided for in Items 2 to 6 of Article 16 of the Criminal Procedure Law in which criminal liability shall not be investigated.

Article 219 After reviewing a case prosecuted by the People's Procuratorate, the People's Court shall handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the case is not within the jurisdiction of this court, it shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(2) If the case falls under the circumstances provided for in Items (2) to (6) of Article 16 of the Criminal Procedure Law, it shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate; if it is a case to be handled only upon complaint, the victim shall simultaneously be informed that he or she has the right to file a private prosecution;
(3) If the defendant is not present, the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate; however, with respect to a case prosecuted by the People's Procuratorate in accordance with the trial in absentia procedure, a decision shall be made in accordance with the provisions of Chapter 24 of this Interpretation;
(4) If the case does not conform to any of Items (2) to (9) of the preceding Article and supplementary materials are needed, the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to provide them within three days;
(5) If, after a defendant is pronounced not guilty in accordance with Item (3) of Article 200 of the Criminal Procedure Law, the People's Procuratorate re-institutes a prosecution based on new facts or evidence, the case shall be accepted according to law;
(6) If a case for which withdrawal of prosecution was permitted by ruling in accordance with Article 296 of this Interpretation is re-prosecuted without new facts or evidence affecting conviction and sentencing, the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(7) If the defendant's true identity is unknown, but the provision of Paragraph 2 of Article 160 of the Criminal Procedure Law is satisfied, the case shall be accepted according to law.
Whether to accept a public prosecution case shall be reviewed and completed within seven days.

Article 221 Before the court session, the People's Court shall carry out the following work:
(1) Determine the presiding judge and the members of the collegial panel;
(2) Serve a copy of the bill of indictment on the defendant and the defender ten days prior to the court session;
(3) Notify the parties, legal representatives, defenders, and litigation agents to provide, five days prior to the court session, the list of witnesses and expert witnesses, and the evidence intended to be produced in court; where an application is made for witnesses, expert witnesses, or persons with specialized knowledge to appear in court, the names, genders, ages, occupations, addresses, and contact information of the relevant persons shall be listed;
(4) Notify the People's Procuratorate of the time and place of the court session three days prior to the court session;
(5) Serve, three days prior to the court session, the summons to summon the parties and the notices to notify the defenders, litigation agents, legal representatives, witnesses, expert witnesses, and other persons to appear in court; the notice to relevant persons to appear in court may also be made by telephone, short message, fax, email, instant messaging, or other means that can confirm receipt; for crimes involving a large number of victims, the relevant documents may be published on the Internet to notify relevant persons to appear in court;
(6) For a case heard in public, announce the cause of action, the defendant's name, and the time and place of the court session three days prior to the court session.
The above work shall be recorded in the case file.

Article 226 Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may decide to convene a pre-trial conference:
(1) The evidentiary materials are numerous and the case is major and complex;
(2) The prosecution and the defense have major disputes over the facts and evidence;
(3) The case has significant social impact;
(4) Other circumstances requiring the convening of a pre-trial conference.

Article 228 At a pre-trial conference, the People's Court may ascertain the circumstances and hear opinions from the prosecution and the defense on the following matters:
(1) Whether there is any objection to the jurisdiction of the case;
(2) Whether to apply for the withdrawal of relevant personnel;
(3) Whether to apply for a non-public hearing;
(4) Whether to apply for the exclusion of illegally obtained evidence;
(5) Whether to provide new evidentiary materials;
(6) Whether to apply for a new expert evaluation or inspection;
(7) Whether to apply for the collection or obtaining of evidentiary materials proving the defendant's innocence or lesser guilt;
(8) Whether to apply for witnesses, expert witnesses, persons with specialized knowledge, investigators, investigation personnel, or other persons to appear in court, and whether there is any objection to the list of persons appearing in court;
(9) Whether there is any objection to the ownership of the property involved in the case and to the handling suggestions of the People's Procuratorate;
(10) Other issues related to the trial.
During the pre-trial conference, the People's Court may conduct mediation of the incidental civil action.
With respect to the procedural matters specified in Paragraph 1 that may cause interruption of the trial, the People's Court may, after the pre-trial conference, handle them according to law and explain the handling decisions and reasons during the trial. If the prosecution or the defense, without new reasons, again raises relevant applications or objections during the trial, the court may, after explaining the circumstances of the pre-trial conference and the reasons for the handling decision, reject them according to law.
The circumstances of the pre-trial conference shall be recorded in written minutes, which shall be checked and signed by the participants.

Article 234 Before the court session, the court clerk shall, in order, carry out the following work:
(1) Upon the entrustment of the presiding judge, verify whether the public prosecutor, parties, defenders, litigation agents, witnesses, and other participants in the proceedings are present;
(2) Verify whether any of the persons attending the hearing are witnesses, expert witnesses, or persons with specialized knowledge;
(3) Invite the public prosecutor, defenders, litigation agents, and other participants in the proceedings to enter the courtroom;
(4) Read out the courtroom rules;
(5) Invite the presiding judge, judges, and people's assessors to enter the courtroom;
(6) After the judicial personnel are seated, report to the presiding judge that the preparations before the trial have been completed.

Article 235 After the presiding judge announces the opening of the court session and summons the defendant into court, the presiding judge shall verify the following circumstances of the defendant:
(1) Name, date of birth, ethnicity, place of birth, educational level, occupation, and address, or, for a defendant unit, its name, domicile, legal representative, actual controller, and the name and position of its litigation representative;
(2) Whether the defendant has previously received criminal punishment, administrative penalty, or disciplinary sanction, and the types and times thereof;
(3) Whether custody has been taken and the time of custody, whether compulsory measures have been taken and the types and times of the compulsory measures;
(4) The date of receipt of the copy of the bill of indictment; if there is an incidental civil action, the date on which the defendant in the incidental civil action received the incidental civil complaint.
If there are multiple defendants, the above circumstances may be verified before the court session, but the presiding judge shall make an explanation at the time of trial.

Article 238 The presiding judge shall inform the parties, their legal representatives, defenders, and litigation agents that, during the court proceedings, they enjoy the following litigation rights according to law:
(1) They may apply for the withdrawal of the members of the collegial panel, judge assistants, court clerk, public prosecutors, expert witnesses, and interpreters;
(2) They may present evidence, apply for the notification of new witnesses to appear in court, apply for the obtaining of new evidence, and apply for a new expert evaluation or inspection;
(3) The defendant may defend himself or herself;
(4) The defendant may make a final statement after the conclusion of the court debate.

Article 253 If a witness is unable to appear in court to testify under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may permit him or her not to appear in court:
(1) He or she suffers from a serious illness or has great difficulty in moving during the period of the trial;
(2) His or her residence is far from the place of hearing and transportation is extremely inconvenient;
(3) He or she is abroad and cannot return to the country within a short period;
(4) There are other objective reasons that truly make it impossible to appear in court.
Under the circumstances provided in the preceding paragraph, testimony may be given by video or other means.

Article 261 Questioning of witnesses shall follow the following rules:
(1) The content of the questioning shall be related to the facts of the case;
(2) Questioning shall not be conducted in a leading manner;
(3) Witnesses shall not be threatened;
(4) The personal dignity of witnesses shall not be impaired.
The provisions of the preceding paragraph apply to the interrogation and questioning of defendants, victims, parties to incidental civil actions, expert witnesses, persons with specialized knowledge, investigators, investigation personnel, or other persons.

Article 276: During the court trial, facts and evidence related to sentencing shall be investigated.
In addition to reviewing whether the defendant has statutory sentencing circumstances, the People's Court shall also, based on the circumstances of the case, review the following circumstances affecting sentencing:
(1) the cause of the case;
(2) whether the victim was at fault and the degree of fault, and whether the victim bore responsibility for the aggravation of the conflict and the extent of such responsibility;
(3) whether the defendant's close relatives assisted in capturing the defendant;
(4) the defendant's usual behavior and whether the defendant has an attitude of repentance;
(5) the circumstances of returning illegal gains, making restitution, and paying compensation;
(6) whether the defendant has obtained the understanding of the victim or the victim's close relatives;
(7) other circumstances affecting sentencing.

Article 281: Court debate shall be conducted under the auspices of the presiding judge in the following order:
(1) speech by the public prosecutor;
(2) speech by the victim and the victim's litigation agent;
(3) the defendant's self-defense;
(4) defense by the defender;
(5) debate between the prosecution and the defense.

Article 295: For first-instance public prosecution cases, after trial, the People's Court shall make judgments or rulings separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) where the facts charged in the prosecution are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, and the crime charged against the defendant is established according to law, a conviction judgment shall be made;
(2) where the facts charged in the prosecution are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, but the charge is improper, a conviction judgment shall be made in accordance with the law and the facts found in the trial;
(3) where the facts of the case are clear, the evidence is reliable and sufficient, and the defendant is found not guilty according to law, a judgment shall be rendered declaring the defendant not guilty;
(4) where evidence is insufficient and the defendant cannot be found guilty, a judgment shall be rendered declaring the defendant not guilty on the grounds that the evidence is insufficient and the charged crime cannot be established;
(5) where part of the facts of the case are clear and the evidence is reliable and sufficient, a judgment of guilty or not guilty shall be made; the parts with unclear facts and insufficient evidence shall not be affirmed;
(6) where the defendant is not subject to criminal punishment because he or she has not reached the age of criminal responsibility, a judgment shall be rendered declaring that the defendant bears no criminal liability;
(7) where the defendant is a person with mental illness who causes harmful consequences when unable to recognize or control his or her own conduct and is exempted from criminal punishment, a judgment shall be rendered declaring that the defendant bears no criminal liability; where the defendant meets the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, the case shall be tried and a judgment shall be made in accordance with the provisions of Chapter 26 of this Interpretation;
(8) where the crime has passed the time limit for prosecution and prosecution is not required, or where the punishment is exempted by a special pardon order, a ruling shall be made to terminate the trial;
(9) where the case is handled only upon complaint, a ruling shall be made to terminate the trial, and the victim shall be informed of the right to bring a private prosecution;
(10) where the defendant dies, a ruling shall be made to terminate the trial; however, if there is evidence proving that the defendant is not guilty and the non-guilt is confirmed through trial in absentia, a judgment shall be rendered declaring the defendant not guilty.
With respect to the property involved in the case, the People's Court shall, according to the circumstances found in the trial, handle it in accordance with the provisions of Chapter 18 of this Interpretation.
Where the circumstance provided in item (2) of the first paragraph exists, the People's Court shall, before the judgment, hear the opinions of the prosecution and the defense, and ensure that the defendant and the defender fully exercise the right to defense. Where necessary, it may hold court again to organize the prosecution and the defense to debate what crime the defendant's conduct constitutes and how to sentence.

Article 306: During the court session, all persons shall obey the direction of the court, observe courtroom discipline, respect judicial etiquette, and shall not commit the following acts:
(1) applauding, making noise, or walking around at will;
(2) smoking or eating;
(3) making or answering phone calls, or using instant messaging tools;
(4) audio recording, video recording, photographing, or disseminating the court session by using instant messaging tools or otherwise;
(5) other acts that endanger courtroom safety or disrupt courtroom order.
Observers shall not enter the trial activity area, shall not stand up or walk around at will, and shall not speak or ask questions.
Journalists who, with permission, engage in the conduct provided in item (4) of the first paragraph shall do so at the designated time and in the designated area, and shall not interfere with the court session.

Article 307: Where a relevant person endangers courtroom safety or disrupts courtroom order, the presiding judge shall, respectively, handle the matter according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the circumstances are relatively minor, the person shall be warned and stopped; depending on the specific circumstances, an admonition may also be given;
(2) if the admonition is ineffective, the person shall be ordered to leave the courtroom; if the person refuses to leave, judicial police shall be instructed to forcefully remove the person from the courtroom;
(3) if the circumstances are serious, after reporting to the president of the court for approval, the person may be imposed a fine of not more than 1,000 yuan or detention of not more than 15 days.
Where, without permission, a person audio records, video records, photographs, or disseminates the court session by using instant messaging tools or otherwise, the relevant equipment and storage media may be temporarily seized and the relevant content deleted.
Where a relevant person refuses to accept the decision on fine or detention, he or she may directly apply to the People's Court at the next higher level for reconsideration, or may apply to the People's Court at the next higher level for reconsideration through the People's Court that decided the fine or detention. Where an application for reconsideration is made through the People's Court that decided the fine or detention, that People's Court shall, within three days after receiving the application for reconsideration, submit the application for reconsideration, the decision on fine or detention, and the relevant facts and evidence materials to the People's Court at the next higher level for reconsideration. During the reconsideration period, the execution of the decision shall not be suspended.

Article 309: Whoever commits any of the following acts, endangering courtroom safety or disrupting courtroom order, and constituting a crime, shall be investigated for criminal liability according to law:
(1) illegally carrying firearms, ammunition, controlled knives, or dangerous substances such as explosive, flammable, poisonous, radioactive, or infectious disease pathogen materials into the courtroom;
(2) creating disturbances or storming the courtroom;
(3) insulting, defaming, threatening, or assaulting judicial staff or litigation participants;
(4) destroying courtroom facilities, or seizing or damaging litigation documents or evidence;
(5) other acts endangering courtroom safety or disrupting courtroom order.

Article 310: Where a defender seriously disrupts the order of the courtroom and is ordered to withdraw from the courtroom, forcibly removed from the courtroom, or punished by fine or detention, and the defendant defends himself/herself, the courtroom trial shall continue; where the defendant requests to retain another defender, or the defendant falls under the circumstances where legal aid shall be provided, the court shall announce an adjournment.
After a defender or litigation agent has been ordered to withdraw from the courtroom, forcibly removed from the courtroom, or punished by fine, if he/she submits a written guarantee ensuring obedience to the court's direction and no further disruption of courtroom order, with the permission of the court, he/she may continue to serve as defender or litigation agent.
A defender or litigation agent shall not continue to serve as defender or litigation agent in the same case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) leaving the courtroom without permission;
(2) failing to appear in court or failing to appear on time without justifiable reasons, seriously affecting the smooth conduct of the trial;
(3) being detained or, after submitting a written guarantee, again being ordered to withdraw from the courtroom or forcibly removed from the courtroom.

Article 316: For the People's Court to accept a private prosecution case, the following conditions must be met:
(1) it conforms to the provisions of Article 210 of the Criminal Procedure Law and Article 1 of this Interpretation;
(2) it falls within the jurisdiction of this court;
(3) the victim brings a complaint;
(4) there is a clear defendant, specific claims, and evidence proving the defendant's criminal facts.

Article 319: A private prosecution complaint shall generally include the following contents:
(1) the name, gender, age, ethnicity, place of birth, educational level, occupation, work unit, address, and contact information of the private prosecutor (person bringing the complaint on behalf of the victim) and the defendant;
(2) the time, place, means, circumstances, harmful consequences, etc., of the defendant's commission of the crime;
(3) the specific claims;
(4) the People's Court to which it is submitted and the date of filing;
(5) the names and sources of evidence, etc.;
(6) the names, addresses, and contact information of witnesses, etc.
Where a complaint is brought against two or more defendants, copies of the private prosecution complaint shall be provided in accordance with the number of defendants.

Article 320: For a private prosecution case, the People's Court shall complete the review within fifteen days. After review, if the acceptance conditions are met, it shall decide to file the case and notify the private prosecutor or the person bringing the complaint on behalf of the victim in writing.
Under any of the following circumstances, the court shall persuade the private prosecutor to withdraw the prosecution; if the private prosecutor does not withdraw the prosecution, it shall rule not to accept the case:
(1) the case is not one provided in Article 1 of this Interpretation;
(2) there is insufficient evidence of the crime;
(3) the limitation period for prosecution has expired;
(4) the defendant is deceased;
(5) the defendant's whereabouts are unknown;
(6) except where the prosecution was withdrawn due to insufficient evidence, after the private prosecutor withdraws the prosecution, he/she brings a complaint again on the same facts;
(7) after the case is concluded through mediation by the People's Court, the private prosecutor reneges and brings a complaint again on the same facts;
(8) the case belongs to the circumstances provided in Item 2 of Article 1 of this Interpretation, and a public security organ is placing the case on file and investigating it, or the People's Procuratorate is reviewing the prosecution;
(9) the private prosecutor, being dissatisfied with the conditional non-prosecution decision made by the People's Procuratorate for a minor criminal suspect, or the non-prosecution decision made after the probation period for conditional non-prosecution expires, brings a lawsuit with the People's Court.

Article 334: In cases handled only upon complaint and minor criminal cases where the victim has evidence to prove the case, the defendant or his/her legal representative may, during the proceedings, file a counterclaim against the private prosecutor. A counterclaim must satisfy the following conditions:
(1) the object of the counterclaim must be the private prosecutor in the present case;
(2) the content of the counterclaim must be conduct related to the present case;
(3) the counterclaim case must conform to the provisions of Items 1 and 2 of Article 1 of this Interpretation.
Counterclaim cases shall be governed by the provisions on private prosecution cases and shall be tried together with the private prosecution case. If the private prosecutor withdraws the prosecution, it shall not affect the continued trial of the counterclaim case.

Article 349: For a case of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment for which the People's Procuratorate initiates a public prosecution, the People's Court shall focus on reviewing the following contents:
(1) whether, when interrogating the criminal suspect, the People's Procuratorate informed him/her of his/her litigation rights and the legal provisions on admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment;
(2) whether transcripts of opinions heard from the criminal suspect, defender, duty lawyer, victim, and the victim's litigation agent are transferred with the case file;
(3) where the defendant and the victim have reached a mediation or settlement agreement or obtained the victim's understanding, whether relevant materials such as the mediation agreement, settlement agreement, and the victim's letter of understanding are transferred with the case file;
(4) where an affidavit of admission of guilt and acceptance of punishment is required to be signed, whether the affidavit is transferred with the case file.
If the materials provided in the preceding paragraph are not transferred with the case file, the People's Procuratorate shall be requested to supplement them.

Article 360: The simplified procedure shall not be applied under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the defendant is blind, deaf, or mute;
(2) the defendant is a mental patient who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his own conduct;
(3) the case has major social impact;
(4) in a joint crime case, some defendants do not admit guilt or object to the application of the simplified procedure;
(5) the defender makes a defense of not guilty;
(6) the defendant admits guilt but upon examination it is considered that the act may not constitute a crime;
(7) other circumstances where the simplified procedure is not suitable to be applied.

Article 365: When trying a case under the simplified procedure, the court session may be simplified as follows:
(1) the public prosecutor may read the indictment in an abridged form;
(2) the questioning and examination of the defendant by the public prosecutor, defender, and judicial personnel may be simplified or omitted;
(3) as to evidence on which both the prosecution and defense have no objection, a statement may be made only of the name of the evidence and the matters it proves; evidence on which the prosecution and defense have objections or which the court considers necessary to investigate and verify shall be produced and subjected to cross-examination;
(4) if the prosecution and defense have no objection to the facts and evidence relevant to conviction and sentencing, the court trial may directly focus on the determination of the charge and the issue of sentencing.
When trying a case under the simplified procedure, the final statement of the defendant shall be heard before the judgment is pronounced.

Article 368: When trying a case under the simplified procedure, if during court trial any of the following circumstances exists, the case shall be transferred to the ordinary procedure:
(1) the defendant's act may not constitute a crime;
(2) the defendant may not bear criminal liability;
(3) the defendant denies in court the criminal facts alleged in the prosecution;
(4) the facts of the case are unclear or the evidence is insufficient;
(5) other circumstances where the simplified procedure should not or is not suitable to be applied.
For a case decided to be transferred to the ordinary procedure, the trial period shall be calculated from the date on which the decision is made.

Article 370: The fast-track adjudication procedure shall not be applied under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the defendant is blind, deaf, or mute;
(2) the defendant is a mental patient who has not completely lost the ability to recognize or control his own conduct;
(3) the defendant is a minor;
(4) the case has major social impact;
(5) in a joint crime case, some defendants object to the alleged criminal facts, the charges, sentencing recommendations, or the application of the fast-track adjudication procedure;
(6) the defendant and the victim or his legal representative have not reached a mediation or reconciliation agreement on matters such as compensation in collateral civil action;
(7) the defender makes a defense of not guilty;
(8) other circumstances where the fast-track adjudication procedure is not suitable to be applied.

Article 375: When trying a case under the fast-track adjudication procedure, if during court trial any of the following circumstances exists, the case shall be transferred to the ordinary procedure or the simplified procedure:
(1) the defendant's act may not constitute a crime or the defendant should not be investigated for criminal liability;
(2) the defendant admits guilt and accepts punishment against his will;
(3) the defendant denies the alleged criminal facts;
(4) the case is difficult or complex, or there is major controversy over the application of law;
(5) other circumstances where the fast-track adjudication procedure is not suitable to be applied.

Article 387: The second-instance people's court shall, with respect to the appeal or protest case file and evidence transferred by the first-instance people's court, examine whether they include the following contents:
(1) a letter transferring the appeal or protest case;
(2) the written appeal or written protest;
(3) eight copies of the first-instance judgment or ruling (plus one copy for each additional defendant) and their electronic texts;
(4) all case files and evidence, including the case trial report and other materials that should be transferred.
If the materials listed in the preceding paragraph are complete, the second-instance people's court shall accept the case; if the materials are incomplete, it shall notify the first-instance people's court to make supplementary delivery in a timely manner.

Article 391: For appeal and protest cases, the following contents shall be examined with emphasis:
(1) whether the facts found in the first-instance judgment are clear and whether the evidence is reliable and sufficient;
(2) whether the law applied in the first-instance judgment is correct and whether the sentence is appropriate;
(3) whether there were any circumstances violating statutory procedures during investigation, criminal investigation, examination and prosecution, and the first-instance proceedings;
(4) whether the appeal or protest raises new facts or evidence;
(5) the situation of the defendant's statements and defenses;
(6) the defender's defense opinions and whether they were adopted;
(7) whether the judgment or ruling on the collateral civil part is lawful and appropriate;
(8) whether the disposition of property involved in the case is correct;
(9) the opinions of the collegiate bench and the adjudication committee of the first-instance people's court.

Article 393: The following cases shall, in accordance with Article 234 of the Criminal Procedure Law, be tried in court:
(1) appeal cases in which the defendant, private prosecutor, or their legal representatives raise objections to the facts or evidence found in the first instance, which may affect conviction and sentencing;
(2) appeal cases in which the defendant is sentenced to death;
(3) cases protested by the people's procuratorate;
(4) other cases that should be tried in court.
Where a defendant sentenced to death does not appeal but other co-defendants in the same case appeal, the second-instance people's court shall try the case in court.

Article 398: When trying an appeal or protest case in court, in addition to mutatis mutandis application of the relevant provisions of the first-instance procedure, the trial shall be conducted in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) at the stage of court investigation, after the judicial personnel read the first-instance judgment or ruling, in appeal cases, the appellant or the defender shall first read the written appeal or state the reasons for appeal; in protest cases, the prosecutor shall first read the written protest; in cases with both appeal and protest, the prosecutor shall first read the written protest, and then the appellant or the defender shall read the written appeal or state the reasons for appeal;
(2) at the stage of court debate, in appeal cases, the appellant and the defender shall speak first, and then the prosecutor and the litigation agent shall speak; in protest cases, the prosecutor and the litigation agent shall speak first, and then the defendant and the defender shall speak; in cases with both appeal and protest, the prosecutor and the litigation agent shall speak first, and then the appellant and the defender shall speak.

Article 399: When trying an appeal or protest case in court, the trial may focus on the issues disputed or the parts in doubt in the first-instance judgment or ruling. Depending on the circumstances of the case, the trial may be conducted in the following ways:
(1) when reading the first-instance judgment, only the cause of action, main facts, names of evidence, and the main text of the judgment may be read;
(2) court investigation shall focus on the facts, evidence, and new evidence with respect to which objections are raised against the first-instance judgment; facts, evidence, and circumstances to which no objection is raised may be directly confirmed;
(3) for defendants in a jointly tried case who did not appeal, if they are not requested to appear in court or the people's court considers it unnecessary for them to appear in court, they may no longer be summoned to appear;
(4) in a case where the defendant has committed several crimes, the crimes for which the facts are clear and no objection is raised may not be tried during the court session.
In a jointly tried case, if a defendant who did not file an appeal and against whose judgment the people's procuratorate did not file a protest requests to appear in court, the request shall be permitted. The defendant appearing in court may participate in court investigation and debate.

Article 401. Where a case is tried in which the defendant or his legal representative, defender, or close relative files an appeal, no judgment substantially unfavorable to the defendant's punishment shall be made on appeal, and the following provisions shall be complied with:
(1) Where, in a case tried jointly, only some of the defendants appeal, the punishment of neither the appellant nor the other co-defendants in the same case may be aggravated;
(2) Where the crime determined in the original judgment is improper, the crime may be changed, but the punishment shall not be aggravated or adversely affected in the execution of the punishment;
(3) Where the number of crimes determined in the original judgment is improper, the number of crimes may be changed and the punishment adjusted, but the punishment decided to be executed shall not be aggravated or adversely affected in the execution of the punishment;
(4) Where the original judgment granted probation to the defendant, the probation shall not be revoked or the probation period extended;
(5) Where the original judgment did not impose an occupational prohibition or a prohibition order, no such imposition shall be added; where the original judgment imposed an occupational prohibition or a prohibition order, its content shall not be increased or its term extended;
(6) Where the original judgment sentenced the defendant to death with a two-year reprieve without restricting commutation or deciding on life-long imprisonment, commutation shall not be restricted or life-long imprisonment decided;
(7) Where the punishment imposed in the original judgment is improper, or a supplementary punishment should have been imposed but was not, the punishment shall not be directly aggravated or a supplementary punishment imposed. Where the punishment imposed in the original judgment is obviously light and must be corrected according to law, a retrial shall be conducted under the procedure for trial supervision after the judgment or ruling of second instance takes effect.
Cases in which the People's Procuratorate files a protest or the private prosecutor files an appeal shall not be subject to the restrictions in the preceding paragraph.

Article 409. Where the People's Court of Second Instance tries a case in which an appeal is filed against the incidental civil part and the criminal part has already taken legal effect, it shall review the entire case and handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the criminal part of the first-instance judgment is not improper, it is only necessary to make a disposition on the incidental civil part;
(2) If the criminal part of the first-instance judgment is indeed erroneous, a retrial of the criminal part shall be conducted under the procedure for trial supervision, and the incidental civil part shall be tried together with the criminal part.

Article 414. A case in which approval is requested from the Supreme People's Court for imposing a punishment below the statutory sentencing range shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the defendant does not appeal and the People's Procuratorate does not file a protest, the case shall be submitted to the people's court at the next higher level for review within three days after the expiration of the appeal or protest period. If the higher people's court agrees with the original judgment, it shall report in writing through the levels to the Supreme People's Court for approval; if it disagrees, it shall rule to remand the case for retrial, or bring the case up for trial under the second-instance procedure;
(2) If the defendant appeals or the People's Procuratorate files a protest, and the people's court at the next higher level upholds the original judgment, or after modifying the judgment still imposes a punishment below the statutory sentencing range, it shall, in accordance with the provisions of the preceding item, report through the levels to the Supreme People's Court for approval.

Article 420. Cases of parole submitted to the Supreme People's Court for approval because the criminal has special circumstances and is not subject to the restriction of the executed term of imprisonment shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) After the Intermediate People's Court makes a parole ruling according to law, it shall submit the ruling to the Higher People's Court for review. If the Higher People's Court agrees, it shall submit a written request to the Supreme People's Court for approval; if it disagrees, it shall rule to quash the parole ruling of the Intermediate People's Court;
(2) Where the Higher People's Court makes a parole ruling according to law, it shall submit the ruling to the Supreme People's Court for approval.

Article 423. Cases submitted to the Supreme People's Court for approval of the death penalty shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) For a first-instance case in which the Intermediate People's Court imposes the death penalty and the defendant does not appeal and the People's Procuratorate does not file a protest, the case shall be submitted to the Higher People's Court for review within ten days after the expiration of the appeal or protest period. If the Higher People's Court agrees to imposing the death penalty, it shall submit the case to the Supreme People's Court for approval within ten days after making its ruling; if it considers that defects exist in a specific fact found in the original judgment or the legal provisions cited, but sentencing the defendant to death is not improper, it may, after correcting the defects, render a judgment or ruling of approval; if it disagrees with imposing the death penalty, it shall bring the case up for trial under the second-instance procedure or remand it for retrial;
(2) For a first-instance case in which the Intermediate People's Court imposes the death penalty and the defendant appeals or the People's Procuratorate files a protest, and the Higher People's Court rules to affirm the judgment, the case shall be submitted to the Supreme People's Court for approval within ten days after making the ruling;
(3) For a first-instance case in which the Higher People's Court imposes the death penalty and the defendant does not appeal and the People's Procuratorate does not file a protest, the case shall be submitted to the Supreme People's Court for approval within ten days after the expiration of the appeal or protest period.
When reviewing a death penalty case, the Higher People's Court shall interrogate the defendant.

Article 426. A report submitted for review of a death penalty or death sentence with a two-year reprieve case shall state the charge, a brief summary of the facts, the trial process, and the result of the judgment.
The comprehensive case report shall include the following contents:
(1) Basic information about the defendant and the victim. If the defendant has a criminal record or has previously been subject to administrative penalty or disciplinary sanction, such facts shall be stated;
(2) The origin and trial process of the case. If the case was previously remanded for retrial, the reasons, time, and case number of the remand for retrial shall be stated;
(3) The circumstances of solving the case. The circumstances in which the defendant was captured and the case was solved through technical investigation or investigative measures, as well as the circumstances relating to the determination of voluntary surrender or meritorious service, shall be stated;
(4) The circumstances of the first-instance trial. Including the opinions of the prosecution and the defense, the criminal facts determined in the first instance, and the opinions of the collegial panel and the judicial committee;
(5) The circumstances of the second-instance trial or the review by the Higher People's Court. Including the reasons for appeal, the opinions of the People's Procuratorate, the facts determined in the second-instance trial or the review by the Higher People's Court, the circumstances of and reasons for the admission of evidence, and the opinions of the prosecution and the defense and whether such opinions were adopted;
(6) Issues that need to be explained. Including the handling of co-offenders in a joint crime case who were dealt with in separate cases, whether the case has major social impact, and the reactions of the parties;
(7) Proposed handling opinions. Stating the opinions of the collegial panel and the judicial committee.

Article 427. In reviewing a case of death penalty or death sentence with a two-year reprieve, the following contents shall be comprehensively examined:
(1) The age of the defendant, whether the defendant has criminal responsibility capacity, and whether the defendant is a pregnant woman;
(2) Whether the facts determined in the original judgment are clear and whether the evidence is reliable and sufficient;
(3) The circumstances, consequences, and degree of harm of the crime;
(4) Whether the law applied in the original judgment is correct, whether the death penalty is necessary, and whether immediate execution is necessary;
(5) Whether there are statutory or discretionary circumstances for heavier, lighter, or mitigated punishment;
(6) Whether the litigation procedure is lawful;
(7) Other circumstances that shall be examined.
In reviewing a case of death penalty or death sentence with a two-year reprieve, attention shall be paid to examining the exculpatory statements and defense opinions of the defendant and his defender.

Article 428. When the Higher People's Court reviews a case of death sentence with a two-year reprieve, it shall handle the case separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts determined and the law applied in the original judgment are correct, the sentence is appropriate, and the litigation procedure is lawful, it shall rule to approve;
(2) If defects exist in a specific fact determined in the original judgment or the legal provisions cited, but sentencing the defendant to death with a two-year reprieve is not improper, it may, after correcting the defects, render a judgment or ruling of approval;
(3) If the facts determined in the original judgment are correct but the law applied is erroneous, or the sentence is excessively heavy, it shall modify the judgment;
(4) If the facts in the original judgment are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, it may rule not to approve, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial, or modify the judgment according to law;
(5) If new facts or evidence affecting conviction and sentencing appear during the review period, it may rule not to approve, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial, or after trial in accordance with the provisions of Article 271 of this Interpretation, modify the judgment according to law;
(6) If the original trial violated the statutory litigation procedure and may affect a fair trial, it shall rule not to approve, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial.
When reviewing a case of death sentence with a two-year reprieve, the punishment of the defendant shall not be aggravated.

Article 429. When the Supreme People's Court reviews a death penalty case, it shall handle the case separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts determined and the law applied in the original judgment are correct, the sentence is appropriate, and the litigation procedure is lawful, it shall rule to approve;
(2) If defects exist in a specific fact determined in the original judgment or the legal provisions cited, but sentencing the defendant to death is not improper, it may, after correcting the defects, render a judgment or ruling of approval;
(3) If the facts in the original judgment are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, it shall rule not to approve, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(4) If new facts or evidence affecting conviction and sentencing appear during the review period, it shall rule not to approve, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(5) If the facts determined in the original judgment are correct and the evidence is sufficient, but the defendant shall not be sentenced to death according to law, it shall rule not to approve, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial; where necessary according to the circumstances of the case, it may also modify the judgment according to law;
(6) If the original trial violated the statutory litigation procedure and may affect a fair trial, it shall rule not to approve, quash the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial.

Article 441: Where physical evidence has not been transferred with the case file, the following contents shall be examined separately according to the circumstances:
(1) For bulk items or items inconvenient to transport, whether the list of sealing and seizure has been transferred with the case file, together with photographs of the original items and sealing formalities, indicating the storage location, etc.;
(2) For items prone to decay, mildew, or difficult to preserve, after the sealing and seizure organ has sold or disposed of them, whether photographs of the original items, lists, and vouchers (copies) for the disposal have been transferred with the case file;
(3) For firearms, ammunition, highly toxic substances, flammable and explosive articles, and other contraband or dangerous articles, after the sealing and seizure organ has disposed of them in accordance with relevant provisions, whether photographs of the original items and lists have been transferred with the case file.
Where the above-mentioned physical evidence not transferred with the case file should be appraised and valued according to law, it shall also be examined whether expert evaluation opinions and valuation opinions are attached.
Where the currency, negotiable securities, etc., that have been sealed and seized have not been transferred as physical evidence, it shall be examined whether photographs of the original items, lists, or other certifying documents are attached.

Article 452: To file a petition with the People's Court, the following materials shall be submitted:
(1) A petition letter, which shall state the basic information of the party, contact information, and the facts and reasons for the petition;
(2) The original legal documents of first and second instance, such as judgments and rulings. Where the case has been reexamined or retried by the People's Court, a notice of rejection of the petition, a retrial decision, a retrial judgment, and a ruling shall be attached;
(3) Other relevant materials. Where the petition is based on new evidence proving that the facts determined by the original judgment or ruling are indeed erroneous, the relevant evidence materials shall be attached at the same time; where application is made to the People's Court for investigation and evidence collection, relevant clues or materials shall be attached.
Where the petition complies with the provisions of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall issue a receipt for receipt of the petition materials. Where the petition does not comply with the provisions of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall notify the petitioner to supplement the materials; if the petitioner refuses to supplement the necessary materials without justifiable reasons, the petition shall not be reviewed.

Article 457: For a petition case placed on file for review, a decision shall be made within three months, and in no case later than six months. Where it is necessary to extend the review period because the case is difficult, complex, major, or for other special reasons, the provisions of Article 210 of this Interpretation shall be applied by reference.
Upon review, if any of the following circumstances exists, it shall be decided to retry the case in accordance with Article 253 of the Criminal Procedure Law:
(1) There is new evidence proving that the facts determined in the original judgment or ruling are indeed erroneous and may affect conviction and sentencing;
(2) The evidence on which the conviction and sentencing were based is not reliable or insufficient and shall be excluded according to law;
(3) There are contradictions among the primary evidence proving the facts of the case;
(4) The primary factual basis has been changed or revoked according to law;
(5) The determination of the charge is erroneous;
(6) The sentencing is obviously inappropriate;
(7) There are obvious errors in the handling of illegal gains or other property involved in the case;
(8) Violating the legal provisions on retroactive effect;
(9) Violating the statutory litigation procedures, which may affect a fair judgment;
(10) The judicial officers committed acts of embezzlement, bribery, engaging in malpractices for personal gain, or perverting the law in adjudicating the case.
Where the petition does not have any of the above circumstances, the petitioner shall be persuaded to withdraw the petition; if the petitioner still insists on the petition, a written notice of rejection shall be given.

Article 458: Evidence that falls under any of the following circumstances and may change the facts on which the original judgment or ruling was based for conviction and sentencing shall be determined as 'new evidence' as provided in Item (1) of Article 253 of the Criminal Procedure Law:
(1) Evidence discovered after the original judgment or ruling took effect;
(2) Evidence discovered before the original judgment or ruling took effect but not collected;
(3) Evidence collected before the original judgment or ruling took effect but not cross-examined;
(4) The expert evaluation opinions, records of inspection, examination, etc., on which the original judgment or ruling was based have been changed or denied;
(5) Evidence such as the defendant's confession and witness testimony on which the original judgment or ruling was based has changed, affecting conviction and sentencing, and there are reasonable grounds for such change.

Article 462: For a case protested by the People's Procuratorate in accordance with the trial supervision procedure, the People's Court shall place the case on file within one month after receiving the written protest. However, under any of the following circumstances, the case shall be handled differently according to the circumstances:
(1) If the case is not within the jurisdiction of that court, the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(2) If the written protest cannot be served on the protested original defendant according to the address provided in the written protest, the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to provide the address of the original defendant within three days; if not provided upon expiration of the time limit, the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(3) If the protest is based on new evidence, but the relevant evidence materials are not attached, or the relevant evidence does not point to the facts of the original prosecution, the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to supplement the relevant materials within three days; if not supplemented upon expiration of the time limit, the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate.
For a protested case decided to be returned, if the People's Procuratorate protests again after supplementing the relevant materials, and upon examination the case meets the acceptance conditions, the People's Court shall accept it.

Article 472: After a retrial case has been retried, it shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the facts determined and the law applied in the original judgment or ruling are correct and the sentencing is appropriate, a ruling shall be made to reject the petition or protest and affirm the original judgment or ruling;
(2) If the conviction in the original judgment or ruling is accurate and the sentencing is appropriate, but there are defects in determining facts or applying law, a ruling shall be made to correct such defects and affirm the original judgment or ruling;
(3) If the facts determined in the original judgment or ruling are not erroneous, but the law applied is erroneous or the sentencing is inappropriate, the original judgment or ruling shall be revoked and a new judgment shall be rendered according to law;
(4) For a case tried in accordance with the procedure of second instance, if the facts in the original judgment or ruling are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, a new judgment may be rendered after the facts are clarified, or a ruling may be made to revoke the original judgment and remand the case to the original trial People's Court for retrial.
If the facts in the original judgment or ruling are unclear or the evidence is insufficient, and the facts have been clarified upon trial, judgment shall be rendered according to law based on the clarified facts; if the facts still cannot be clarified and the evidence is insufficient to establish the defendant's guilt, the original judgment or ruling shall be revoked and a judgment shall be rendered acquitting the defendant.

Article 475: For the purposes of this Interpretation, 'foreign-related criminal cases' mean:
(1) Cases in which a foreigner commits a crime within the territory of the People's Republic of China, or a Chinese citizen commits a crime against a foreign country or a foreigner;
(2) Cases in which a Chinese citizen commits a crime outside the territory of the People's Republic of China under the circumstances provided for in Articles 7 and 10 of the Criminal Law;
(3) Cases in which a foreigner commits a crime under the circumstances provided for in Articles 8 and 10 of the Criminal Law;
(4) Cases in which the People's Republic of China exercises jurisdiction within the scope of international treaty obligations it undertakes under the circumstances provided for in Article 9 of the Criminal Law.

Article 480 Where it is necessary to notify the relevant embassy or consulate of the country concerned stationed in China of relevant matters, the matter shall be reported level by level to the Higher People's Court, which shall make the notification in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) Where the country of nationality of the foreign national party has concluded a bilateral consular treaty with China, the matter shall be handled in accordance with the treaty provisions; where no bilateral consular treaty has been concluded with China, but the country is a party to the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, the matter shall be handled in accordance with the provisions of the Convention; where no consular treaty has been concluded with China and the country is not a party to the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, but has diplomatic relations with China, the matter may be handled in accordance with the opinions of the foreign affairs department, the principle of reciprocity, and relevant provisions and international practice;
(2) For a foreign-related criminal case occurring within the consular district of a foreign consulate in China, the relevant foreign consulate in that region shall be notified; for a foreign-related criminal case occurring outside the consular district of a foreign consulate, the relevant foreign embassy in China shall be notified; where a country has diplomatic relations with China but has not established an embassy or consulate, the embassy or consulate in China of the state representing its interests may be notified; where there is no representing state, or the representing state is unknown, notification may be omitted;
(3) Where a bilateral consular treaty stipulates a time limit for notification, notification shall be made within the prescribed time limit; where there is no such stipulation, notification shall be made as soon as possible in accordance with or by reference to the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations and international practice, and shall not exceed seven days at the latest;
(4) Where a bilateral consular treaty does not stipulate that notification is mandatory, and the foreign national party requests that his or her country of nationality's embassy or consulate in China not be notified, notification may be omitted, but the party shall personally issue a written statement.
Where the Higher People's Court notifies the relevant foreign embassy or consulate in China of relevant matters, it may, if necessary, request the assistance of the foreign affairs department of the people's government.

Article 495 Where the People's Court serves criminal procedural documents on a party residing outside the territory of the People's Republic of China, the following methods may be used:
(1) Service in accordance with the method prescribed by the international treaty concluded between the country where the addressee is located and the People's Republic of China or to which both countries are parties;
(2) Service through diplomatic channels;
(3) For a party of Chinese nationality, where the law of the country where the party is located permits or the country agrees, the embassy or consulate of China in the country where the addressee is located may be entrusted to serve the documents on his or her behalf;
(4) Where the party is a private prosecutor in a case of private prosecution or a plaintiff in an incidental civil action, service may be made on a litigation agent who is authorized to accept service on his or her behalf;
(5) Where the party is a foreign entity, service may be made on its representative office established within the territory of the People's Republic of China or its branch or business agent authorized to accept service;
(6) Where the law of the country where the addressee is located permits, service may be made by mail; where three months have elapsed since the date of mailing and the receipt of service has not been returned, but it is sufficient to determine that service has been effected based on the various circumstances, it shall be deemed served;
(7) Where the law of the country where the addressee is located permits, service may be made by fax, e-mail, or other means capable of confirming receipt by the addressee.

Article 500 Where the lower People's Court, after receiving the execution order for the death penalty and before execution, discovers that any of the following circumstances exists, it shall suspend the execution and immediately report the report requesting suspension of execution of the death penalty and relevant materials level by level to the Supreme People's Court:
(1) The criminal may have committed other crimes;
(2) Other criminal suspects in the joint crime have been brought to justice, which may affect the sentencing of the criminal;
(3) Other criminals in the joint crime have been suspended or stopped from execution of the death penalty, which may affect the sentencing of the criminal;
(4) The criminal has exposed major criminal facts or has other major meritorious service, which may necessitate a change of sentence;
(5) The criminal is pregnant;
(6) There may be other errors in the judgment or ruling affecting the conviction and sentencing.
Where the Supreme People's Court, upon examination, considers that it may affect the conviction and sentencing of the criminal, it shall rule to stop the execution of the death penalty; where it considers that it does not affect, it shall decide to continue the execution of the death penalty.

Article 504 The Supreme People's Court shall handle a case in which the execution of the death penalty has been stopped in accordance with the following circumstances respectively:
(1) Where it is confirmed that the criminal is pregnant, the sentence shall be changed;
(2) Where it is confirmed that the criminal has committed other crimes and shall be prosecuted according to law, it shall rule not to approve the death penalty, revoke the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(3) Where it is confirmed that the original judgment or ruling is erroneous or the criminal has major meritorious service and a change of sentence is necessary, it shall rule not to approve the death penalty, revoke the original judgment, and remand the case for retrial;
(4) Where it is confirmed that the original judgment or ruling is not erroneous, the criminal has no major meritorious service, or the major meritorious service does not affect the execution of the original judgment or ruling, it shall rule to continue the execution of the death penalty, and the president shall reissue the execution order for the death penalty.

Article 510 After the execution of the death penalty, the People's Court responsible for the execution shall handle the following matters:
(1) The criminal's suicide note and record of last words shall be promptly examined; where they involve property succession, debt settlement, family matters entrusted, etc., the suicide note and record of last words shall be handed over to the family members, with copies kept in the file for future reference; where they involve case clues or other issues, copies shall be sent to the relevant organs;
(2) The criminal's family members shall be notified to collect the criminal's ashes within a time limit; where there are no cremation conditions or cremation is inappropriate due to ethnic, religious, or other reasons, the family members shall be notified to collect the body; where the ashes or body are not collected upon expiration of the time limit, the People's Court shall notify the relevant unit to handle the matter and require the relevant unit to issue an explanation of the handling; the handling of the criminal's ashes or body shall be recorded in the case file;
(3) After the execution of the death penalty on a foreign national criminal, the procedures and time limits for notifying the foreign embassy or consulate in China shall be handled in accordance with relevant provisions.

Article 516 After receiving the written recommendation for execution of imprisonment from the community correction institution, the People's Court shall, upon examination, make a decision to execute imprisonment if it confirms that a criminal serving a sentence temporarily outside prison falls under any of the following circumstances:
(1) failing to meet the conditions for temporary service of sentence outside prison;
(2) leaving the city or county where he or she resides without approval and, after being warned, refusing to correct, or refusing to report whereabouts and evading supervision;
(3) having been subjected to public security administration penalty for violating supervision and administration regulations and still refusing to correct;
(4) having received two warnings from the executing organ and still refusing to correct;
(5) during the period of medical parole, failing to submit the re-examination of the illness in accordance with the provisions and, after being warned, refusing to correct;
(6) after the circumstances for temporary service of sentence outside prison disappear, the term of imprisonment has not expired;
(7) the surety loses the qualifications for guarantee or is disqualified as a surety due to failure to perform obligations, and cannot propose a new surety within the prescribed time limit;
(8) other circumstances where laws, administrative regulations, and supervision and administration regulations are violated and the circumstances are serious.

Article 521 Enforcement of the property-related part of a criminal judgment refers to the enforcement of the following items in a criminal judgment that has taken legal effect:
(1) fines and confiscation of property;
(2) recovery and ordering of restitution of illegal gains;
(3) disposition of illicit money and goods transferred with the case;
(4) confiscation of the criminal's own property used for the crime and transferred with the case;
(5) other property-related judgments that shall be enforced by the People's Court.

Article 529 In the process of enforcing the property-related part of a criminal judgment or an incidental civil judgment, if any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall rule to terminate the enforcement:
(1) the judgment or ruling based on which enforcement is conducted has been revoked;
(2) the person subject to enforcement dies or is executed with the death penalty and has no property available for enforcement;
(3) the entity sentenced to a fine terminates and has no property available for enforcement;
(4) the fine is exempted in accordance with Article 53 of the Criminal Law;
(5) other circumstances under which enforcement shall be terminated.
After a ruling to terminate enforcement, if it is discovered that the property of the person subject to enforcement has been concealed or transferred, the same shall be recovered.

Article 534 Cases of commutation of punishment and parole shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) Commutation of punishment for a criminal sentenced to death with a two-year reprieve shall be ruled by the Higher People's Court at the place where the criminal serves his or her sentence within one month after receiving the written recommendation for commutation that has been examined and approved by the prison administration organ at the same level;
(2) Commutation of punishment and parole for a criminal sentenced to life imprisonment shall be ruled by the Higher People's Court at the place where the criminal serves his or her sentence within one month after receiving the written recommendation for commutation or parole that has been examined and approved by the prison administration organ at the same level, which may be extended by one month if the case is complex or the circumstances are special;
(3) Commutation of punishment and parole for a criminal sentenced to fixed-term imprisonment and for a criminal whose sentence has been commuted to fixed-term imprisonment shall be ruled by the Intermediate People's Court at the place where the criminal serves his or her sentence within one month after receiving the written recommendation for commutation or parole proposed by the executing organ, which may be extended by one month if the case is complex or the circumstances are special;
(4) Commutation of punishment for a criminal sentenced to public surveillance or criminal detention shall be ruled by the Intermediate People's Court at the place where the criminal serves his or her sentence within one month after receiving the written recommendation for commutation that has been examined and approved by the executing organ at the same level.
Commutation of punishment for a person under community correction shall be ruled by an Intermediate People's Court or a higher People's Court at the place where community correction is executed within thirty days after receiving the written recommendation for commutation from the community correction institution.

Article 535 When accepting a case of commutation of punishment or parole, it shall be examined whether the materials transferred by the executing organ include the following:
(1) the written recommendation for commutation or parole;
(2) copies of the judgment documents of the original trial court, the execution notice, and previous rulings on commutation of punishment;
(3) written materials proving the specific facts of the criminal's genuine repentance, meritorious service, or major meritorious service;
(4) the criminal's evaluation and appraisal form, and forms for approval of rewards and punishments;
(5) an investigation and evaluation report on the impact of the criminal's parole on the community where he or she will reside;
(6) the enforcement and performance of the property-related part of the criminal judgment and the incidental civil judgment;
(7) other materials that need to be transferred according to the circumstances of the case.
Where the People's Procuratorate submits opinions on a case submitted for commutation or parole, the executing organ shall transfer such opinions together to the People's Court accepting the case.
Upon examination, if the materials are incomplete, the executing organ that proposed the commutation or parole shall be notified to supplement the materials within three days; if not supplemented within the time limit, the case shall not be placed on file.

Article 537 In hearing a case of commutation of punishment or parole, the following matters shall be publicized within five days after the case is placed on file:
(1) the basic personal information of the criminal, such as name and age;
(2) the crime and term of sentence determined by the original judgment;
(3) the criminal's previous commutations of punishment;
(4) the executing organ's recommendation for commutation or parole and the basis therefor.
The publicity notice shall state the publicity period and the manner of submitting opinions.

Article 538 In hearing a case of commutation of punishment or parole, a collegial panel shall be formed, and written examination may be used; however, the following cases shall be heard in court:
(1) cases where commutation of punishment is proposed because the criminal has performed major meritorious service;
(2) cases where the starting time, interval, or extent of commutation proposed does not conform to the general provisions;
(3) cases where the criminal for whom commutation of punishment or parole is proposed is a criminal of duty-related crime, a criminal who organizes, leads, participates in, shields, or connives at an organization of a mafia-type nature, a criminal who disrupts financial management order, or a criminal of financial fraud;
(4) cases with major social impact or high social concern;
(5) cases where different opinions are received during the publicity period;
(6) cases where the People's Procuratorate raises an objection;
(7) other cases in which it is necessary to hold a hearing in court.

Article 543 After receiving the written recommendation for revocation of probation from the community correction institution, the People's Court shall, upon examination, make a ruling to revoke probation if it confirms that the criminal falls under any of the following circumstances during the probation period:
(1) violating a prohibition order with serious circumstances;
(2) without justifiable reasons, failing to report within the prescribed time or evading supervision during community correction for more than one month;
(3) having been subjected to public security administration penalty for violating supervision and administration regulations and still refusing to correct;
(4) having received two warnings from the executing organ and still refusing to correct;
(5) other circumstances where laws, administrative regulations, and supervision and administration regulations are violated and the circumstances are serious.
After receiving the written recommendation for revocation of parole from the community correction institution, the People's Court shall, upon examination, make a ruling to revoke parole if it confirms that the criminal falls under any of the circumstances provided in items 2 and 4 of the preceding paragraph during the parole period, or has other acts violating supervision and administration regulations that do not yet constitute a new crime.

Article 550 Cases in which the defendant was under the age of eighteen when committing the alleged crime and under the age of twenty when the People's Court placed the case on file shall be heard by an adjudication organization for cases involving minors.
The following cases may be heard by an adjudication organization for cases involving minors:
(1) criminal cases of enrolled students under the age of twenty-two when the People's Court places the case on file;
(2) criminal cases infringing upon the personal rights of minors, such as rape, indecent assault, abuse, and abandonment of minors;
(3) other cases more suitable to be heard by an adjudication organization for cases involving minors.
In a joint crime case involving a minor defendant or in other criminal cases involving minors, whether to be heard by an adjudication organization for cases involving minors shall be decided by the president of the court according to the actual circumstances.

Article 598: For a case prosecuted by the People's Procuratorate in accordance with the provisions of Article 291, paragraph 1 of the Criminal Procedure Law, the People's Court shall focus on reviewing the following contents:
(1) Whether it falls within the scope of cases to which the trial by default procedure may be applied;
(2) Whether it is within the jurisdiction of this court;
(3) Whether the basic information of the defendant is stated, including the definite overseas residence, contact information, etc.;
(4) Whether the main facts of the crime suspected by the defendant are stated, with evidentiary materials attached;
(5) Whether it is stated whether the defendant has close relatives and the names, identities, addresses, contact information, etc. of the close relatives;
(6) Whether the types, quantities, values, locations, etc. of illegal gains and other property involved in the case are listed, with evidentiary materials attached;
(7) Whether a list and relevant legal formalities for sealing, seizing, and freezing illegal gains and other property involved in the case are attached.
Where the materials specified in the preceding paragraph require translations, the People's Court shall require the People's Procuratorate to transfer them together.

Article 599: After review of a case prosecuted by the People's Procuratorate in accordance with Article 291, paragraph 1 of the Criminal Procedure Law, the People's Court shall handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If it meets the conditions for application of the trial by default procedure, is within the jurisdiction of this court, and the materials are complete, it shall accept the case;
(2) If it does not fall within the scope of cases to which the trial by default procedure may be applied, is not within the jurisdiction of this court, or does not meet other conditions for application of the trial by default procedure, it shall return the case to the People's Procuratorate;
(3) If the materials are incomplete, it shall notify the People's Procuratorate to supplement them within thirty days; if they cannot be supplemented within thirty days, it shall return the case to the People's Procuratorate.

Article 609. The criminal cases of "crimes of embezzlement and bribery, terrorist activities, and the like" as prescribed in Article 298 of the Criminal Procedure Law refer to the following cases:
(1) cases of duty-related crimes such as embezzlement, bribery, dereliction of duty, and malfeasance;
(2) cases of crimes related to terrorist activities as prescribed in Chapter II of the Specific Provisions of the Criminal Law, as well as criminal cases such as homicide, explosion, and kidnapping committed by terrorist organizations or terrorist personnel;
(3) cases of crimes endangering national security, smuggling, money laundering, financial fraud, crimes committed by mafia-type organizations, and drug-related crimes;
(4) cases of telecom fraud and internet fraud.

Article 612. With respect to an application for confiscation of illegal gains filed by the People's Procuratorate, the People's Court shall examine the following contents:
(1) whether the case falls within the scope of cases to which the procedure for confiscation of illegal gains is applicable;
(2) whether it is within the jurisdiction of this court;
(3) whether the basic information of the criminal suspect or defendant and the circumstances of the alleged crimes are stated, and evidence materials are attached;
(4) whether the circumstances such as the criminal suspect's or defendant's fleeing, being wanted, escaping, disappearance, or death are stated, and evidence materials are attached;
(5) whether the types, quantities, values, locations, and the like of the illegal gains and other property involved in the case are listed, and evidence materials are attached;
(6) whether a list and legal formalities for sealing, seizing, and freezing the illegal gains and other property involved in the case are attached;
(7) whether it is stated whether the criminal suspect or defendant has interested parties, and the names, identities, addresses, contact information, and claims of the interested parties, and the like;
(8) whether the reasons and legal basis for the application for confiscation are stated;
(9) other contents and materials that need to be examined according to law.
Where the materials specified in the preceding paragraph require translation, the People's Court shall require the People's Procuratorate to submit the translations together.

Article 613. The People's Court shall complete the examination of an application for confiscation of illegal gains within thirty days and handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) where the application falls within the scope of acceptance of applications for confiscation of illegal gains and the jurisdiction of this court, and the materials are complete and there is evidence proving the existence of criminal facts, the application shall be accepted;
(2) where the application does not fall within the scope of acceptance of applications for confiscation of illegal gains or the jurisdiction of this court, the application shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(3) where the application for confiscation of illegal gains does not meet the standard requirement of "there is evidence proving the existence of criminal facts," the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to withdraw the application;
(4) where the materials are incomplete, the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to submit supplementary materials within seven days; if the materials cannot be supplemented within seven days, the application shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate.
Where the People's Procuratorate has not yet sealed, seized, or frozen the property for which confiscation is applied, or the duration of sealing, seizure, or freezing is about to expire, and there is a risk that the property involved in the case may be concealed, transferred, damaged, or destroyed, the People's Court may seal, seize, or freeze the property for which confiscation is applied.

Article 614. After accepting an application for confiscation of illegal gains, the People's Court shall publish an announcement within fifteen days. The announcement shall specify the following contents:
(1) the cause of action and the source of the case;
(2) the basic information of the criminal suspect or defendant;
(3) the facts of the crime of which the criminal suspect or defendant is suspected;
(4) the circumstances such as the criminal suspect's or defendant's fleeing, being wanted, escaping, disappearance, or death;
(5) the types, quantities, values, locations, and the like of the property for which confiscation is applied, as well as the list and legal formalities for the property already sealed, seized, or frozen;
(6) the relevant facts that the property for which confiscation is applied constitutes illegal gains and other property involved in the case;
(7) the reasons and legal basis for the application for confiscation;
(8) the time limit and method for interested parties to apply to participate in the proceedings, and the adverse legal consequences that may be borne if they fail to apply for participation within such time limit or in such manner;
(9) other circumstances that shall be announced.
The announcement period is six months, and the provisions on suspension, interruption, and extension shall not apply during the announcement period.

Article 620. A case involving an application for confiscation of illegal gains heard in court shall proceed according to the following procedures:
(1) after the chief judge announces the commencement of the court investigation, the prosecutor shall first read the application, and then the interested parties and litigation agents shall present their opinions;
(2) the court shall, in turn, investigate whether the criminal suspect or defendant committed major crimes such as embezzlement, bribery, or terrorist activities and had been wanted for one year without appearing, or whether he or she has died, and whether the property for which confiscation is applied shall be recovered according to law; during the investigation, the prosecutor shall first present evidence, and then the interested parties and litigation agents shall present evidence and conduct cross-examination;
(3) during the court debate stage, the prosecutor shall first speak, and then the interested parties and litigation agents shall speak and debate.
Where an interested party, after receiving the notice, refuses to appear in court without justifiable reasons, or withdraws from the court session midway without the permission of the court, the case may be converted to a trial without a court session, except where other interested parties participate in the proceedings.

Article 623. For appeal or protest cases against a first-instance ruling to confiscate illegal gains or to reject the application, the second-instance People's Court shall, after trial, handle them separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) where the facts found in the first-instance ruling are clear and the law applied is correct, the appeal or protest shall be dismissed and the original ruling affirmed;
(2) where the facts found in the first-instance ruling are clear but the law applied is erroneous, the original ruling shall be modified;
(3) where the facts found in the first-instance ruling are unclear, the original ruling may be modified after the facts are ascertained, or the original ruling may be revoked and the case remanded to the original trial court for retrial;
(4) where the first-instance ruling violates the statutory litigation procedures and may affect an impartial trial, the original ruling shall be revoked and the case remanded to the original trial court for retrial.
After the first-instance People's Court renders a ruling on a remanded case, the second-instance People's Court shall, for an appeal or protest against the ruling of the first-instance People's Court, render a ruling according to law and shall not remand the case to the original trial court for retrial again, except where the first-instance People's Court violates the statutory litigation procedures during the retrial and may affect an impartial trial.

Article 632. With respect to an application for compulsory medical treatment filed by the People's Procuratorate, the People's Court shall examine the following contents:
(1) whether it is within the jurisdiction of this court;
(2) whether the identity of the respondent, the time, place, means, and resulting harm of the violent conduct, and other circumstances are stated, and evidence materials are attached;
(3) whether an expert opinion on forensic psychiatric evaluation and other evidence materials proving that the respondent is a mental patient who is not criminally liable according to law are attached;
(4) whether the name, address, and contact information of the respondent's legal representative are listed;
(5) other matters that need to be examined.

Article 633. The People's Court shall complete the examination of an application for compulsory medical treatment filed by the People's Procuratorate within seven days and handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) where the application falls within the scope of acceptance of the compulsory medical treatment procedure and the jurisdiction of this court, and the materials are complete, the application shall be accepted;
(2) where the application is not within the jurisdiction of this court, it shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate;
(3) where the materials are incomplete, the People's Procuratorate shall be notified to submit supplementary materials within three days; if the materials cannot be supplemented within three days, the application shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate.

Article 636. A case involving an application for compulsory medical treatment tried in court shall proceed according to the following procedures:
(1) after the chief judge announces the commencement of the court investigation, the prosecutor shall first read the application, and then the legal representative and litigation agent of the respondent shall present their opinions;
(2) the court shall, in turn, investigate whether the respondent committed violent conduct endangering public safety or seriously endangering the personal safety of citizens, whether the respondent is a mental patient who is not criminally liable according to law, and whether the respondent is likely to continue to endanger society; during the investigation, the prosecutor shall first present evidence, and then the legal representative and litigation agent of the respondent shall present evidence and conduct cross-examination; where necessary, an expert witness may be notified to appear in court to explain the expert opinion;
(3) during the court debate stage, the prosecutor shall first speak, and then the legal representative and litigation agent of the respondent shall speak and debate.
Where the respondent requests to appear in court, and the People's Court, after examining his or her physical and mental condition, considers that it is appropriate for the respondent to appear, the court shall permit it. A respondent who appears in court may present opinions during the court investigation and debate stages.
After the prosecutor reads the application, if the legal representative and litigation agent of the respondent raise no objection, the court investigation may be simplified.

Article 637. After hearing a case involving an application for compulsory medical treatment, the People's Court shall handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) where the conditions for compulsory medical treatment prescribed in Article 302 of the Criminal Procedure Law are met, a decision shall be made to subject the respondent to compulsory medical treatment;
(2) where the respondent is a mental patient who is not criminally liable according to law but does not meet the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, a decision shall be made to reject the application for compulsory medical treatment; where the respondent has already caused harmful consequences, his or her family members or guardians shall, at the same time, be ordered to keep strict custody and provide medical treatment;
(3) where the respondent has full or partial criminal liability capacity and criminal liability shall be investigated according to law, a decision shall be made to reject the application for compulsory medical treatment, and the case shall be returned to the People's Procuratorate for handling according to law.

Article 639: For a case specified in the preceding Article, after trial, the People's Court shall, according to the following circumstances, handle it separately:
(1) Where the defendant meets the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, it shall pronounce a judgment declaring that the defendant bears no criminal liability, and at the same time make a decision on compulsory medical treatment for the defendant;
(2) Where the defendant is a mental patient who bears no criminal liability according to law but does not meet the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, it shall pronounce a judgment declaring the defendant not guilty or bearing no criminal liability; if the defendant has already caused harmful consequences, it shall at the same time order his or her family members or guardian to keep strict watch and provide medical treatment;
(3) Where the defendant has full or partial criminal capacity and shall be investigated for criminal liability according to law, the trial shall continue in accordance with the ordinary procedure.

Article 643: For an application for reconsideration of a compulsory medical treatment decision, the People's Court at the next higher level shall form a collegial panel to hear the case, and shall, within one month, make a reconsideration decision according to the following circumstances:
(1) Where the person subject to compulsory medical treatment meets the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, the reconsideration application shall be rejected and the original decision upheld;
(2) Where the person subject to compulsory medical treatment does not meet the conditions for compulsory medical treatment, the original decision shall be revoked;
(3) Where the original trial violated the statutory litigation procedures and may affect the impartiality of the trial, the original decision shall be revoked and the case remanded to the original trial People's Court for retrial.
